    "targetCode": "nodicsDocsNodePagecommerceFulfillmentCoreSourceMap",
    "title": "Fulfillment Core Source Map",
    "summary": "Exact source map for fulfillment execution, carrier adapters, return execution, integration readiness, customer policy, operator evidence, and recovery.",
    "searchText": "Fulfillment Core Source Map Exact source map for fulfillment execution, carrier adapters, return execution, integration readiness, customer policy, operator evidence, and recovery. # Fulfillment Core Source Map\n\nFulfillment Core owns shipping, carrier execution, return execution, integration readiness, and customer-safe fulfillment actions. The broader Fulfillment page explains the business journey; this source map shows developers, operators, QA owners, and AI tools where the implementation lives. For beginners, this is the place where an order becomes work that can be picked, shipped, returned, or blocked with clear evidence.\n\n## Business problem\n\nThe business problem is trustworthy order execution. After checkout, the business must prove which carrier was selected, whether integration was ready, whether a shipment failed, and whether a return can be accepted. Fulfillment Core keeps execution state near the services that own fulfillment behavior, while product, payment, and identity authority stay in their own modules.\n\n## Source map\n\n| Area | Source location |\n| --- | --- |\n| Fulfillment Core module | `.` |\n| Schemas and routes | `src/schemas/schemas.js`, `src/router/routers.js` |\n| Customer controller | `src/controller/defaultFulfillmentCustomerController.js` |\n| Lifecycle and operations | `src/service/defaultFulfillmentLifecycleService.js`, `src/service/defaultFulfillmentOperationService.js` |\n| Carrier execution | `src/service/defaultCarrierExecutionService.js` |\n| Return execution | `src/service/defaultFulfillmentReturnExecutionService.js` |\n| Reviewed physical Order reversal and manual operations | `src/service/defaultPhysicalOrderReversalService.js`, `src/controller/defaultPhysicalFulfillmentController.js` |\n| Connected physical owner contract and adverse tests | `llm/contracts/physical-order-reversal.md`, `test/physicalOrderReversalContract.test.js` |\n| Readiness checks | `src/service/defaultFulfillmentIntegrationReadinessService.js` |\n| Contract tests | `test` |\n| ITEM delivery evidence refusal boundary | src/service/defaultFulfillmentItemDeliveryEvidenceService.js, llm/contracts/verified-item-delivery.md, test/fulfillmentItemDeliveryEvidenceContract.test.js |\n| Explicit LOCAL-only ITEM simulation | src/service/defaultFulfillmentItemSimulationService.js, config/properties.js, test/fulfillmentItemSimulationContract.test.js; simulated/unverified/nonimmutable projection only |\n\n## Execution flow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  Order[Original Order and retained consignment] --> Policy[Explicit Store selection and current Profile employee scope]\n  Policy --> Dispatch[Reviewed manual handover]\n  Dispatch --> Stock[Inventory original hold SHIP proof]\n  Stock --> Shipment[Owner-issued shipment]\n  Policy --> Approval[Reviewed Order cancellation or return approval]\n  Approval --> Physical[Physical prepare and consignment CAS lock]\n  Shipment --> Physical\n  Physical --> Receipt[Return only: full actual receipt and inspection]\n  Physical --> Cancel[Cancellation only: pre-dispatch original holds]\n  Receipt --> Settle[Inventory atomic original-stock reversal]\n  Cancel --> Settle\n  Settle --> Payment[Payment original-capture refund]\n  Payment --> Complete[Physical and Order completion or retained reconciliation]\n```\n\n## Contract\n\nFulfillment Core records identify original Order, consignment, owner-issued shipment, actual package receipt, immutable inspection and safe recovery state without duplicating catalogue, Payment or Profile authority. The reviewed physical bridge is DefaultPhysicalOrderReversalService, not the legacy convenience return-record writer. Generic lifecycle actions and body-supplied shipment/inspection fields cannot substitute for its retained evidence. Read fulfillment.shipping-management, physical-cancellation-return-owner-bridge and physical-return-receipt-inspection for the full business process. Manual attestation, offline carrier conformance and live provider qualification are different evidence classes.\n\n```js\nconst receiptCommand = {\n  confirmed: true,\n  reason: 'Returned package physically received and reviewed',\n  receiptReference: 'actual-package-reference',\n  lines: [{ reservationCode: 'original-order:original-entry', quantity: '1' }]\n};\n// Submit against the original approved return case after actual reception.\n// The command is a manual attestation, not a replacement shipment or approval.\n```\n\n## Customization and extension guidance\n\nDevelopers can add carrier adapters, warehouse allocation rules, return inspection policies, integration readiness checks, customer-facing actions, or operator recovery commands. Business users should see fulfillment progress, blocked setup, and safe retry actions through Axis. Operators need production evidence for integration status, retry count, carrier reference, and return receipt. QA should test successful execution, blocked readiness, carrier failure, retry, return approval, and customer policy boundaries.\n\n## Operating rules\n\nEvery fulfillment extension should declare its stable code, owning module, supported states, retry behavior, and operator evidence before it is enabled for a tenant. Import data can seed carriers, rules, and policies, but services must still validate whether an action is currently allowed. Axis can expose commands such as retry or approve return only when the backend reports that the command is available.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Treating Fulfillment Core as product, payment, or identity authority.\n- Calling a carrier before readiness checks pass.\n- Showing raw carrier errors to customers.\n- Creating return execution without original order and receipt evidence.\n- Adding provider-specific logic without tests and operator recovery data.\n\n## Verification\n\nRun fulfillment customer policy, integration readiness, and return execution contracts. In a fresh schema, place a controlled order, start fulfillment, force a carrier failure, retry, and run a return path. Production readiness requires safe status, source traceability, operator evidence, and QA proof that failed execution does not corrupt order state.\n\nPaths in this source map are relative to the fulfillmentCore module. Its optional documentation pack supplies guidance and CMS records only; it does not install carriers, create orders, grant operator permissions or qualify an external fulfillment integration.\n\nITEM delivery currently has no authenticated original Shipment allocation receipt source. Its exported evidence owner always refuses; physical dispatch/manual handover and generic DELIVERED tracking cannot be described as successful ITEM fulfillment. Read fulfillment.shipping-management#fulfillment-item-delivery-boundary for exact limits and the non-mutating refusal flow. Source coverage and isolated tests are not installed or native E2E qualification. A separately selected LOCAL_SIMULATION evaluator exercises existing merchant orchestration without physical delivery, inventory or receipt issuance. Its SIM: handles, SIMULATED_ITEMS stage and verified:false/immutable:false result must not be promoted by a generic workflow DELIVERED status. Read fulfillment.shipping-management#fulfillment-local-item-simulation and #fulfillment-sim-receipt-boundary. Already-redeemed benefit inverses remain disabled.\n",
